1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,320
And we know that you can be lonely in a crowd and you can be lonely in a marriage.
我们知道，身处人群中也会孤独，身在婚姻中也会孤独。

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:10,600
So the second big lesson that we learned is that it's not just the number of friends you have.
所以，我们学到的第二个重要结论是：关键不只是你有多少朋友。

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:19,519
And it's not whether or not you're in a committed relationship but it's the quality of your close relationships that matters.
也不在于你是否处于稳定的伴侣关系，而在于亲密关系的质量。

4
00:00:19,519 --> 00:00:24,280
It turns out that living in the midst of conflict is really bad for our health.
研究者发现，生活在冲突之中对健康很不利。

5
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:34,000
High conflict marriages for example without much affection turn out to be very bad for our health perhaps worse than getting divorced.
例如，他说，冲突频繁又缺乏关爱的婚姻，对健康很不利，可能比离婚更糟。

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,880
And living in the midst of good warm relationships is protective.
而生活在良好、温暖的关系之中，按他的解释具有保护作用。

7
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:45,000
Once we had followed our men all the way into their 80 s we wanted to look back at them at midlife.
当我们追踪这些男性直到八十多岁时，我们想回头看看他们在中年时的情况。

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,560
And to see if we could predict who was going to grow into a happy healthy oxygenarian and who wasn't.
看能否预测，谁会成为快乐、健康的八十多岁老人，谁不会。

9
00:00:52,560 --> 00:01:04,519
And when we gathered together everything we knew about them at age 50 it wasn't their middle aged cholesterol levels that predicted how they were going to grow old.
把他们五十岁时已知的信息汇总后，预测他们如何老去的，并不是中年的胆固醇水平。

10
00:01:04,519 --> 00:01:07,960
It was how satisfied they were in their relationships.
而是他们对自己人际关系的满意程度。

11
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:16,640
The people who were the most satisfied in their relationships at age 50 were the healthiest at age 80.
在五十岁时对关系最满意的人，到了八十岁时也最健康。

12
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:23,359
And good close relationships seem to buffer us from some of the slings and arrows of getting old.
他还解释，良好的亲密关系似乎能缓冲衰老带来的一些打击和困难。

13
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:35,320
Our most happily partnered men and women reported in their 80 s that on the days when they had more physical pain their moods stayed just as happy.
那些伴侣关系最幸福的男女，在八十多岁时报告说，即使某天身体疼痛更多，心情仍然一样愉快。

14
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:45,320
But the people who were in unhappy relationships on the days when they reported more more physical pain it was magnified by more emotional pain.
而关系不幸福的人，在报告身体疼痛更多的日子，还会受到更多情绪痛苦的放大影响。

15
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:55,399
And the third big lesson that we learn about relationships on our health is that good relationships don't just protect our bodies they protect our brains.
我们学到的第三个有关关系与健康的结论是，良好关系不仅保护身体，也保护大脑。

16
00:01:55,399 --> 00:02:03,910
It turns out that being in a securely attached relationship to another person in your 80 s is protective.
他解释说，在八十多岁时与另一个人建立安全依恋的关系，具有保护作用。

17
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:10,040
That the people who are in relationships where they really feel they can count on the other person in times of need.
处在这类关系中的人，确实觉得自己在需要时可以依靠对方。

18
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:13,800
Those those people's memories stay sharper longer.
这些人的记忆会保持敏锐更久。

19
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:18,560
And the people in relationships where they feel they really can't count on the other one.
而有些关系中的人，觉得自己确实无法依靠对方。

20
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:23,480
Those are the people who experience earlier memory decline.
他们就是更早出现记忆衰退的那些人。

21
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,920
And those good relationships they don't have to be smooth all the time.
而这些良好关系，也不需要时时刻刻都一帆风顺。

22
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:32,240
Some of our octogenarian couples could bicker with each other day in and day out.
研究中有些八十多岁的夫妇，也会日复一日地拌嘴。

23
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:37,320
But as long as they felt that they could really count on the other when the going got tough.
但只要他们觉得，在困难时确实可以依靠彼此——

24
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:42,870
Those arguments didn't take a toll on their memory.
这些争执就没有对他们的记忆造成损害。

25
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:54,040
So this message that good close relationships are good for our health and well being this is wisdom that's as old as the hills.
所以，良好亲密关系有益于健康和幸福这个信息，本是一种古老得不能再古老的智慧。

26
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:58,600
Why is this so hard to get and so easy to ignore.
为什么它这么难做到，又这么容易被忽视呢？

27
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:08,279
Well we're human what we'd really like is a quick fix something we can get that'll make our lives good and keep them that way.
因为我们是人。我们真正想要的是立竿见影的办法，能立刻得到某样东西，让生活变好并一直保持下去。

28
00:03:08,279 --> 00:03:21,480
Relationships are messy and they're complicated and the the hard work of tending to family and friends that's not sexy or glamorous it's also lifelong it never ends.
但关系混乱而复杂；花心力维护家人与朋友的关系，这种艰苦工作并不时髦，也不光鲜，而且贯穿一生，永远没有终点。
